WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:07.180
Imagine what would happen if you left a type
of bird — for example, finches — alone

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:12.030
on a lush planet without predators for hundreds
of millions of years.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.760
What kinds of incredible forms might evolve?

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.339
If you consider that mammals grew from a few
rodent-like species into elephants, whales,

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:26.999
horses, bats, and primates in a much shorter
period after the extinction of dinosaurs—

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:31.419
you’ll start to realize just how incredible
things might get.

00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:36.199
This concept is the idea behind one of the
most detailed speculative biology projects

00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:40.370
ever created; Serina, the World of Birds.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:46.440
This epic multi-million year journey was created
by Dylan Bajda, an incredible artist ad writer.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.809
I have links below where you can follow and
support his work.

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:54.309
There’s so much incredible detail to this
project, and I won’t be able to cover it

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:55.850
all in a single video.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.489
But for now, let’s get started at the very
beginning of the saga, with the introduction

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:03.179
of life on the moon of Serina.

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:09.380
The story begins when some force—perhaps
aliens or advanced humans—places earth lifeforms

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.780
on the moon of Serina.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.710
Specifically, they put... canaries — finches
to be exact — into a terraformed ecosystem

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:15.710
much like earth’s.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:21.409
A few other species — including grasses,
algae, flowering plants like sunflowers, tiny

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:26.590
fish like guppies and swordtails, and insects
like ants and crickets — are also left on

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:32.100
the planet to create a stable food source
for the finches — which are the only large

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.060
land animals on this entire moon.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:40.890
And then, the creators of this mysterious
experiment depart, and evolution takes over.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.969
The explosion in canary diversity is almost
instantaneous.

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:50.920
Badjda speculates that lacking predators and
with a near endless food supply, the population

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.189
would skyrocket over the first ten thousand
years.

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:59.369
From here, early specialization emerges — with
insect-eaters, plant-eaters, egg-eaters, and

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:03.430
seed-eaters all becoming subtly divergent
over generations.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.600
In the sea, guppies and swordtails grow and
diversify.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.220
Over the next several million years, which
is called the Hypostecene, the small fish

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.660
will become larger and more streamlined — with
hydrodynamics shaping them into familiar forms.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.140
Several million years in, and leafcutter ants
have quickly proliferated, with their numbers

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.540
reaching tens of billions.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.780
Soon, larger flightless variants of canaries
emerge to feed on this emerging food source.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:40.010
As the sun rises 10 million years in, a true
ecosystem of different species is forming.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:46.130
Here, the thick-beaked axbills strut across
the plains, near the large womblers and long-legged

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.130
aardgeese.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.290
Already, the moon of Serina is a much different
place from when these birds first arrived.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.130
And from here, things will only get stranger…

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:02.670
11 million years in, an era call the Tempuscene
begins, marked by a steady cooling and an

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.670
increase in arid environments.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.810
At fifteen million years, a region called
the Northern Drylands comprises the largest

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.740
temperate desert region on this moon, where
all sorts of new species are emerging.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.870
It’s now early spring here, and a large
and lithe aardgoose offshoot is strutting

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.830
across the Drylands — keeping an eye on
the fearsome descendants of the formerly herbivorous

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:27.080
Axbills called the Skykes, which are now sharp
billed omnivores.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:33.310
Nearby, one of the first true predators, the
small Falconary, hunts a songbird under the

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.100
branches of huge, tree-like sunflower descendants.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.900
But below them all, in a series of tunnels,
an even more interesting dynamic is unfolding.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.480
These tunnels are made by an enormous lobster-like
arthropod, which despite weighing as much

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.790
as a small cat, is in fact a cricket.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.270
And with it in its home are strange little
birds called ground serins.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.600
They can’t dig tunnels themselves, but make
use of the friendly crickets’ burrow to

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.660
keep themselves safe, and in return act as
lookouts.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.600
A very strange set of roommates indeed.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:08.610
As the Tempuscenic Era progresses, Serina
continues to cool.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:13.690
In this changing environment, the artist imagines
one successful branch of Aardgeese, called

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:19.669
the ‘vivas,’ is on the path of a surprising
adaptation: the birth of live young without

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:21.370
requiring an egg.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:26.139
In the coming ice age, this adaptation will
prove vital to the branch’s success.

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:31.330
Away from the mainland, however, the isolated
Kyran Islands are not yet feeling the effects

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.370
of the global cooling.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:38.180
On these islands — where food grows in abundance
and predators are scarce — very different

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:43.490
selective pressures are creating some of the
largest, and strangest, species of birds so

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:44.490
far.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:50.449
Reaching heights of over ten feet, the chubbirds
are huge offshoots of finches that don’t

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:53.889
resemble any other birds on Serina at this
time.

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:58.510
In this paradise, there’s simply nothing
big enough on these islands to bother them.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.020
In time, though, the islands change.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.509
The chubbirds pass on, and in their place
an even stranger species of megafauna emerge

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:08.879
— better suited for the islands’ new grassier
ecosystem.

00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:12.430
These are the snuffalos: shaggy, heavyset
creatures almost the size of cattle — and

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.350
with a similar grazing behavior.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.349
The snuffalos are so heavy, however, that
they can’t support their weight on their

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:23.211
two feet alone, and must also rely on their
beak for balance, which has become almost

00:05:23.211 --> 00:05:24.770
a third foot.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:25.770
Inefficient?

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.960
Perhaps, but there are few predators on these
islands that would make the snuffalo require

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:30.960
speed.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.860
Save perhaps, for one.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.350
The butcherraptor is an evolutionary newcomer
to the world of Serina, and not large enough

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.539
to seem like a threat to something suffalo-sized.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:44.500
But the butcherraptor has something the snuffalo
does not: intelligence.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:49.280
Butcherraptors are quite possibly the smartest
creatures Serina has yet to produce, efficient

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.880
pack hunters that can, in a group, take down
something as big as a snuffalo.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.980
And in the oceans, life is also diversifying
into unexpected forms.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:04.200
Take, for example, the Swordsharks: predators
whose swordtail ancestors averaged six inches

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.200
in length.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.470
The swordshark, in contrast, averages up to
eight feet — and preys on vast shoals of

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.630
smaller fish, thrashing them with their unique
tails to stun them.

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:19.069
An even more incredible transformation has
occurred in the case of the Giant Gemnus,

00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:23.139
a guest entry into Serina conceptualized by
Joseph Cawley.

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:29.180
The Giant Gemnus grows over thirty feet, or
9 meters in length, and consumes over 400

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.660
pounds, or 180 kilos of plant-matter a day.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.749
And yet, the ancestors of these herbivorous
behemoths… are the tiny guppies.

00:06:37.749 --> 00:06:42.610
Truly aquatic birds called Bloons also emerge
during the Tempuscene — like the starry

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:47.659
Pelecarrier, a species that incubates eggs
under its hind legs and carries its young

00:06:47.659 --> 00:06:49.449
safely in its mouth.

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:54.259
Right now, these early seafaring birds are
far from the largest things in the sea: about

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:56.360
the size of a human diver.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.310
In the future, however, the circumstances
of the Bloons would change…

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:05.180
However, it’s not the Bloons, nor even the
Giant Gemnus that one should be keeping an

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.180
eye on long-term.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.509
These tiny, unfortunate, almost-toad looking
creatures are called mudwickets.

00:07:11.509 --> 00:07:16.199
They’re a species of fish that lives in
tidal areas and are beginning the process

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:18.259
of crawling onto land.

00:07:18.259 --> 00:07:22.790
Right now, they aren’t up too much, but
there are many millions of years to go in

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:24.949
our great epic...

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:30.139
Looking back on Serina’s progress, 50 million
years have now passed since the introduction

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:34.349
of the canaries, and the moon is now entering
a period called the Cryocene.

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:40.810
The climate, already cooling in the Tempuscene,
has now cooled further and over half the moon’s

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:42.520
surface is locked in ice.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:47.659
In this new frontier, the live-bearing vivas
group have come to dominate all manner of

00:07:47.659 --> 00:07:51.449
niches: both at the equator and in the colder
regions.

00:07:51.449 --> 00:07:56.029
From the large topical shimmersnoot vivas
who eat insects with their long tongues, to

00:07:56.029 --> 00:08:00.949
the lithe herbivorous serilopes and thick-beaked
carnivorous banshees of the plains, to the

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:05.610
tiny bumblets who burrow underground to keep
warm — the vivas have become one of the

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.319
most diverse groups in this new world.

00:08:08.319 --> 00:08:13.779
Another successful vivas is the Canaribou
— large, thick-coated offshoots of serilopes

00:08:13.779 --> 00:08:18.900
that live in enormous migratory herds and
can withstand the harsh polar winters in this

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:19.900
region.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:25.499
The Canaribou is the first true live-bearing
bird, giving birth to fully hatched infants.

00:08:25.499 --> 00:08:30.999
Here, a mother protects its newborn from a
Giant Falconry — aerial predators that have

00:08:30.999 --> 00:08:35.080
grown fully gigantic over the ages, but not
so large they’re ready to tangle with an

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:37.380
angry mother protecting its young.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.890
In the tall forests of pineflowers (plants
which have evolved conifer-like needles),

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.840
however, the more familiar descendants of
egg-laying species hold on.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.680
In this more temperate region, the giant flightless
anvilbeak, with a beak that helps it crack

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:56.680
open all manner of seeds, lives near its cousins,
the swordbills — who are lightweight hunters.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:01.530
These holdovers are the distant descendants
of the axbills, and in these forests, their

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.940
lineage will endure through the cooling trends.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:10.500
Another familiar (though increasingly strange)
species are the molebirds — the descendants

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.580
of burrowing finches who, instead of relying
on the tunnels of others, are capable diggers

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:14.830
in their own right.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.440
For millions of years they’ve quietly tunneled
underground, content to be ignored by the

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.560
above ground megafauna.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:25.880
Also much changed since our last visit are
the aquatic Bloons, which are now one of the

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.300
most specialized lineages of birds on Serina.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:34.580
Growing to more than sixty feet (18 meters)
in length and weighing up to fifteen thousand

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:41.070
pounds (6800 kg) the artist describes them
as a family of gentle, herbivorous leviathans

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.310
— and the largest birds to have ever existed.

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:44.220
This particular species, the Emperor Bloons,
have been nicknamed the ‘birdwhales’ — and

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:45.620
you can likely imagine why.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:50.350
And at the water’s edge, the unassuming
mudwickets have continued to adapt, spending

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.270
more and more time on land.

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:57.280
They still don’t look like much, but soon,
circumstances will change...

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.790
But the time of the mudwickets is not yet
upon us.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:05.430
Jumping forwards to the late Cryocene — the
final period we’ll be covering in this particular

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:09.310
video — we’ve arrived at an age…. of
giants.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:14.500
On the plains, one large offshoot of the serilope
are the flutterboks, colorful herbivores with

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.750
a colorful plumage similar in many ways to
the feathers of earth’s peacocks.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:23.150
The flutterboks are in turn stalked by the
dinosaur-like Trunksnouts — whose newly

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:29.030
evolved pseudo-teeth make them effective predators…
with a high degree of evolutionary potential.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:34.160
Even more dinosaur-like are the Serestriders,
the largest terrestrial bird that has yet

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:35.850
to evolve on Serina.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:41.570
Exceeding 12 meters or 40 feet in height and
weighing several tons, the Serestriders are

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:45.910
the sauropods of their day, adapted to browse
the highest branches of trees.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:46.910
Here, a solitary bull serestrider wanders
peacefully in the shade — content in the

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:47.910
knowledge that few species can match it.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.390
But larger herbivores mean larger carnivores.

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:56.020
The Tyrant Serins, the grandest of all descendants
of the distant axbeak, are apex predators

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:59.050
that can take down other large megafauna.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:03.690
Equipped with serrated beaks able to shatter
bone, these tyrants are the heaviest land

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.760
predators yet to evolve.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.380
This period is an early peak of megafauna
on Serina — which now supports species so

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:17.700
large and distinct they’re almost unrecognizable
when one reflects on the moon’s first, humble

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:18.910
inhabitants.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:23.680
Over the next several million years, Serina
would change once again, as an increase in

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.470
volcanic eruptions on a larger scale than
ever before would turn the environment on

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:35.100
its head, giving rise to far more alien-looking
creatures than ever before, which we’ll

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:36.860
explore in our next installment.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.880
But for now, that’s all the time we have.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.240
For now, if you enjoyed learning about the
moon of Serina, consider following and supporting

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.540
Dylan Bajda using the links below.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.620
Also, thanks for watching.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:52.810
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:55.920
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.060
See you in the next video.

